{"text": "Cyl\n\n\u2018Tobacco Cunpany\n\nG. ROBERT DI MARCO\nSenior Viee President\nResearch & Development\n\nWinston-Salem, N.C. 27102\n\nFebruary '27, 1992\n\nMs. Elaine Haemisegger\nStratospheric Ozone Protection Branch\nGlobal Change Division\nOffice of Atmospheric and Indoor Air Programs\nOffice of Air and Radiation, ANR-445\n401 M Street, SW\nWashington, DC 20460\n\nDear Ms. Haemisegger:\nSubject: Petition to EPA under the Significant New Alternatives Policy\n(SNAP) for Favorable Review of HCFC-123 As a Safe Alternative\nfor CFC-11 in the Use Sector of Tobacco Expansion\nUse Sector: Tobacco Expansion\ncurrent Expansion Agent: CFC-11\n\nSubstitute: HCFC-123\nChemical Name: 2;,2-Dichloro-1,1,1-trifluoroethane\n\nCAB No.t 306-83-2\nODP: 0.02\nHGWP: 0.02\nvoc Status:  HCFC-123 is not listed by EPA as a voc\n\nSubstitute Application:\n\nThe CFC-11 tobacco expansion process is a patented, physical process which\nuses CFC-11 to restore cured, aged tobacco to its original field volume.\nThe CFC-11 process is an effective and nonhazardous method of expanding\ntobacco and is currently a widely used tobacco expansion process. In the\nCFC-11 process, tobacco is impregnated with CFC-11 in a stainless steel\nvessel maintained at 120\u00b0F and pressurized to 20 psig. The tobacco is\nthen contacted with hot air (330\u00b0F) which causes the tobacco to regain its\noriginal volume. The CFC-11 is vaporized and recovered by cooling and\ncompressing. Since the CFC-11 is continually recovered and recycled, it\n\n\u201cWe work for = mo}\n\n8169 6gLes",
